From 5fc5ec774de9585610505e9f4466d55e61506601 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: william arzt <bill@tlon.io>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 06:42:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] urwasm: keep-alive: survive aborted events, commit memory as
 used

Review fixes for keeping wasm instances alive between calls.

- Event-abort signals (SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGVTALRM) are held off while
  the C heap is in use or a slot changes hands, and a slot is made
  unmatchable and forgets its buffers before they are freed. A jump out
  of free() or of a half-done teardown can no longer leave a ready slot
  pointing at freed memory, or free one twice.
- u3m_soft_top's signal recovery now calls u3we_lia_reap(), which drops
  the instances the aborted calls were running on. This replaces the
  guess from stack addresses, which could keep an abandoned slot busy
  for good (and, past the budget, refuse every later admission).
- Without real entropy for the per-process id, no instances are kept:
  calls load their state the ordinary way.
- The code and memory reservations are mapped PROT_NONE and opened up
  in 1 MiB steps as they fill, so only what is used counts against a
  strict overcommit limit.
- A new instance gets its compile-order buffer (room for every
  function) and its memory before anything runs, and code space that
  cannot be had while it is being built sends the call back too: these
  failures take the ordinary path instead of bailing mid-call. Only
  growth while wasm runs (memory.grow, lazy compilation) can still bail
  %meme, as the loom would on the ordinary path.
- After a call, an instance too big to keep beside the running ones is
  dropped first, rather than after evicting every other instance.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
